// Fresh project: everything ready for development, proven by the owner's checks.
// Vars: peer, folder, project, build, test, approver
claim: BOOT-1 The toolchain and dependencies {{project}} needs are set up and a minimal program builds
claim: BOOT-2 The repository has a README with prerequisites, setup, build, test and run steps
claim: BOOT-3 There is a unit test, and CI builds and tests every push and pull request
claim: BOOT-4 The project's claims file lists what must be true and the checks that prove it
claim: BOOT-5 The team lead approved the setup
prereqs @{{peer}}:{{folder}} skill=bootstrap-project verify={{build}} attempts=3 claims=BOOT-1 save=setup For {{project}}: detect (or, for an empty folder, choose) the language and toolchain, add the package manifest and lock file, and make a minimal program that builds. List the exact tool versions and commands you used.
readme after prereqs @{{peer}}:{{folder}} skill=bootstrap-project verify={{build}} claims=BOOT-2 Add a README.md (purpose, prerequisites with versions, setup, build, test, run) and a .gitignore for this toolchain.
ci after readme @{{peer}}:{{folder}} skill=bootstrap-project verify={{test}} attempts=3 claims=BOOT-3 Add one meaningful unit test and a CI workflow (.github/workflows/ci.yml) that builds and runs the tests on pushes and pull requests.
claims after ci @{{peer}}:{{folder}} skill=bootstrap-project verify={{test}} claims=BOOT-4 save=claims Create evidence/claims.json: the project's first claims (id, statement, and the tests or checks that prove each), matching the code as it is now.
signoff after claims approve @{{approver}} claims=BOOT-5 Review the setup of {{project}}: toolchain, README, CI and claims.